1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,690
Right. Specifically within your ministry's PAERA- Mm.

2
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:05,910
Uh, the enterprise architecture framework.

3
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:14,090
Yeah, exactly. And the mission here is, like, figuring out how to map out the current state of these really complex internal systems.

4
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:20,310
Mm-hmm. You know, finding the actual gaps without just drowning in some massive five hundred page audit.

5
00:00:20,390 --> 00:00:24,890
Exactly. Because the goal isn't to just list every single thing wrong with your program.

6
00:00:24,950 --> 00:00:28,370
I mean, you really wanna find the right problems, uh, in the right order.

7
00:00:28,710 --> 00:00:32,160
But wait, doesn't an exhaustive audit actually give you a better baseline?

8
00:00:32,590 --> 00:00:38,010
Like, if you document every single asset and process, isn't that a better foundation for decision-making?

9
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:40,850
Uh, no, not really. Quality definitely beats volume here.

10
00:00:40,939 --> 00:00:45,030
An exhaustive audit usually just obscures the actual structural realities of your program.

11
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:47,130
Oh, so you kinda miss the forest for the trees.

12
00:00:47,170 --> 00:00:54,710
Right. So instead of just documenting everything, the current state picture is evaluated against three universal tests across all the architectural layers.

13
00:00:54,770 --> 00:00:57,190
First up, it has to be complete enough to decide.

14
00:00:57,229 --> 00:00:59,610
Complete enough to decide. Meaning what exactly?

15
00:00:59,630 --> 00:01:04,310
Meaning it only covers the capabilities and systems that materially impact the minister's objective.

16
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:06,490
Minor elements, you just note them and move on.

17
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:07,730
Got it. And the second test.

18
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:14,460
It must be owned. Every single capability, data domain, and application needs a named owner.

19
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:15,850
Because if nobody owns it...

20
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:24,210
Exactly. If it's unowned, well, no department is budgeting for its maintenance or security, which is a critical finding, not just some minor oversight.

21
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:27,010
Yikes. Yeah, that makes sense. So what is the third test?

22
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:30,830
The third test is that the architecture has to be traceable.

23
00:01:30,850 --> 00:01:40,410
Say you have a digital service, right? If you can't trace that back up to a specific ministry capability, uh, that is a huge functional gap in understanding how the enterprise actually

24
00:01:40,490 --> 00:01:41,030
operates.

25
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:45,980
Okay, so applying those three universal tests reveals the baseline structural issues.

26
00:01:46,610 --> 00:01:50,430
But we also have to apply specific tests to each architectural layer, right?

27
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:53,510
Mm-hmm. Like business, data application, and technology.

28
00:01:53,810 --> 00:02:02,010
Yeah, exactly. For example, testing the business layer means you have to identify actual capabilities, like what the ministry does, say, issuing permits,

29
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:06,110
rather than just, you know, mapping out organizational departments on a chart.

30
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:10,850
Oh, right. And for the data layer, you'd look for a single authoritative copy of information, right?

31
00:02:10,910 --> 00:02:20,610
You hit the nail on the head. And when we apply these specific layer tests across a government enterprise, we almost always uncover four specific

32
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:21,539
functional failures.

33
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:24,930
Oh, wow. Four reliable disasters just hiding in the architecture.

34
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:25,690
What are they?

35
00:02:25,770 --> 00:02:28,770
So first is the duplicate register. We see this all the time.

36
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:36,110
Multiple regulatory bodies keeping conflicting copies of the exact same data domain, like citizen addresses or tax statuses.

37
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:38,910
Oh, that sounds like an absolute mess. Well, what's the second one?

38
00:02:39,090 --> 00:02:40,290
Orphan systems. Yeah.

39
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:45,430
These are those older legacy applications that literally trace to no current capability.

40
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:49,310
Wait, so they just sit there and consume budget without actually doing anything useful for your program?

41
00:02:49,530 --> 00:02:52,710
Basically, yes. They deliver zero operational value.

42
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:56,470
The third gap is point to point spaghetti architecture.

43
00:02:56,730 --> 00:02:58,270
Point to point spaghetti. I love that term.

44
00:02:58,450 --> 00:03:05,910
It's very visual. Right. That's where systems talk through custom individual links instead of just using a shared data exchange building block.

45
00:03:06,030 --> 00:03:08,930
Right, which sound incredibly fragile. And the fourth gap.

46
00:03:09,170 --> 00:03:16,590
The fourth is having no clear owner for a shared capability, which means when that specific capability fails to perform,

47
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:19,110
accountability just quietly vanishes.

48
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:21,050
Right, because nobody wants to take the blame for it.

49
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:26,750
But look, documenting duplicate registers or orphan systems, that basically just generates a list of findings.

50
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:29,250
How do you convert that into a true phase two assess?

51
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:32,970
Well, you have to sequence those gaps for remediation.

52
00:03:33,010 --> 00:03:36,120
Prioritizing them is the core output of the assessment.

53
00:03:36,790 --> 00:03:39,110
But wait, establishing priority sounds highly political.

54
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:45,750
Like, what if naming a redundant register directly challenges the jurisdiction of some really powerful department within the ministry?

55
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:55,840
Well, that is the reality of the work. Priority comes down to two things: the magnitude of negative impact on your minister's program or the operational cost to the ministry weighed against

56
00:03:55,930 --> 00:03:58,510
how feasible it is to actually close that gap.

57
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:04,890
So you wanna target high impact vulnerabilities that are actually viable to fix, both politically and technically.

58
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:12,250
Exactly. But when this assessment finally goes up to the enterprise architecture board for sign off, the ultimate defining metric is honesty.

59
00:04:12,530 --> 00:04:12,970
Honesty.

60
00:04:13,810 --> 00:04:15,550
Meaning you can't sugarcoat the findings.

61
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:23,430
Right. If you soften the language about a redundant register just to accommodate internal sensitivities, you completely compromise the baseline.

62
00:04:23,590 --> 00:04:25,690
So flattery gets you nowhere in this phase.

63
00:04:25,850 --> 00:04:26,690
Worse than nowhere.

64
00:04:27,959 --> 00:04:36,950
An assessment that flatters the current state will just result in a severely underfunded and fundamentally flawed modernization program the very next year.

65
00:04:37,490 --> 00:04:39,670
It leads to quiet, expensive failures.

66
00:04:39,770 --> 00:04:45,210
Wow. Yeah, that makes total sense. So a phase two assess is absolutely not about volume.

67
00:04:45,270 --> 00:04:50,910
It's about getting an owned traceable picture and a very honestly ordered gap analysis.

68
00:04:51,050 --> 00:04:52,310
That is the perfect summary.

69
00:04:52,770 --> 00:04:53,050
Awesome.

70
00:04:53,670 --> 00:04:56,130
Well, that wraps up our deep dive for today.

